The last large blocks that were freed are kept, and malloc hands one out
again to a request it fits (ADR 0810). frigg's slab_pool maps every
block above 32 KiB by itself and unmaps it when it is freed, so a
program that allocates a large block, frees it and allocates one of the
size again, as one that draws a picture a frame does, paid a mapping, a
page fault for each page and an unmapping every time: 300 faults a
frame for a window of 640 by 480. glibc pays them once: a freed mapping
of up to 32 MiB raises its threshold, and blocks of that size come from
its heap from then on.

The pool keeps at most four freed large blocks, of 16 MiB in all with
their mappings' padding, the one kept longest going back to the system
first. A request takes the smallest kept block that holds it with at
most a quarter as much again left over; malloc_usable_size says what
the block has. A block larger than 16 MiB, or placed on an alignment
above a page, is unmapped when it is freed, as before. Meant for
upstream, in frigg.

